# Ownership of the constitution.
#
# WHY (2026-08-22, topic 52222). The constitutional direction guard cannot
# constrain the agent it governs: the agent holds `push` on this repository,
# the `main` ruleset carried no pull_request rule, and the agent authors both
# the checker and the workflow that runs it. The Ed25519 key the guard trusts
# is subject to the same problem, because that key is a file in this repository.
#
# No check the agent writes can restrain the agent. Only repository settings
# can — they need admin, which the agent does not hold (verified: a ruleset
# write returns 404 for its identity). This file is the path-scoped half.
#
# TWO LINES, and that is deliberate. An earlier draft listed six, adding the
# checker scripts and .github/** on the theory that breaking the checker would
# let a registry change slip past. That theory is WRONG: the approval is
# enforced by GitHub against the FILE PATH, not by the checker. Break the
# checker all you like — a pull request touching the constitution still stops
# and waits. Owning the checker bought nothing and cost the operator approvals
# on unrelated work. Measured over the last 72 commits on main, the six-path
# version would have asked for ~10 approvals; this asks for ~1.
#
# NOT COVERED, on the operator's explicit instruction: .github/** . A workflow
# change can reach RELEASE_TOKEN, documented in publish.yml as a PAT that
# bypasses branch protection. That is a real attack path and this file does not
# close it. Excluded because it is a far more elaborate attack, a different
# problem from constitutional approval, and guarding it would cost an approval
# every week or two on ordinary work. Named here so the omission reads as a
# decision rather than an oversight.
#
# Full reasoning: docs/specs/standards-approval-via-github-review.md

